##----------------------------------------------------------------------##
#
# Errors and leaks to suppress by default, common to all crypto backends
#
# The entries in this file target defects in third-party libraries
# that trigger independently of the crypto backend in use. They are
# loaded in addition to the per-backend suppression files
# (valgrind-openssl.supp, valgrind-nss.supp, valgrind-gcrypt.supp,
# valgrind-gnutls.supp).
#
# Format of this file is:
# {
#     name_of_suppression
#     kind: one of Param Value1 Value2 Value4 Value8
#                   Free Addr1 Addr2 Addr4 Addr8
#                   Cond (previously known as Value0)
#     (if Param: name of system call param, if Free: name of free-ing fn)
#     caller0 name, or /name/of/so/file.so
#     caller1 name, or ditto
#     (optionally: caller2 name)
#     (optionally: caller3 name)
#  }

##----------------------------------------------------------------------##

##
## libxml2: the catalog resolve cache is never released
##
## When a non-local (e.g. http://) URI is resolved through the default
## entity loader, libxml2 lazily creates a global URI-to-location
## resolve cache (the static variable xmlCatalogResolveCache in
## catalog.c). The hash table and the cached key strings are kept
## allocated until process exit: neither xmlCatalogCleanup() nor
## xmlCleanupParser() releases the cache (verified with libxml2 2.15.2),
## and no public API is provided to do so.
##
## As a result, every xmlsec1 invocation that resolves a non-local URI
## reports a small "still reachable" residual at exit (732-737 bytes in
## 4 blocks: the hash structure, the hash table and the cached key
## strings). The cache is intentionally retained by libxml2 to speed up
## repeated URI resolutions, so this is not an xmlsec1 leak; the
## residual is suppressed here.
{
   xmlCreateURLParserCtxt -> xmlACatalogResolveURI -> malloc
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: reachable
   fun:malloc
   ...
   fun:xmlACatalogResolveURI
   ...
   fun:xmlCreateURLParserCtxt
}
